**One Line Summary**
Lang & Schwarz AG is currently facing significant adverse actions, including an ongoing German tax evasion prosecution and a finalized Austrian regulatory fine for market manipulation.

**Findings**
*   **Regulatory & Legal Enforcement:** The Düsseldorf-Mitte tax office is demanding up to €61 million in reassessments from the firm due to its active involvement in the Cum-Ex dividend stripping scandal between 2007 and 2011.[1] In a separate enforcement action, the Austrian Financial Market Authority (FMA) finalized a €154,184 fine against the institution for market manipulation after it negligently executed 19 fictional transactions.
    *   Sources: [https://www.kantenwein.de/fileadmin/images/pdf/Germany.pdf](https://www.kantenwein.de/fileadmin/images/pdf/Germany.pdf) [1] and [https://www.fma.gv.at/en/announcement-fma-imposes-a-sanction-against-lang-schwarz-ag-due-to-breach-of-the-ban-on-market-manipulation/](https://www.fma.gv.at/en/announcement-fma-imposes-a-sanction-against-lang-schwarz-ag-due-to-breach-of-the-ban-on-market-manipulation/)
*   **Executive & Key Person Risk:** The legacy liability for the Cum-Ex tax evasion operations is directly tied to former leadership, specifically former management board member Peter Zahn, whose tenure from July 2004 to October 2019 completely encompassed the investigated trading window.[2]
    *   Source: [https://laiqon.com/fileadmin/user_upload/LAIQON/03_Unternehmen/Investor_Relations/Finanzberichte_Dokumente/LAIQON-GB_2022_EN_geschuetzt.pdf](https://laiqon.com/fileadmin/user_upload/LAIQON/03_Unternehmen/Investor_Relations/Finanzberichte_Dokumente/LAIQON-GB_2022_EN_geschuetzt.pdf) [2]
